      "createdAt": "2026-04-24T12:32:49Z",
      "path": "storage/src/test/java/org/apache/kafka/storage/internals/log/LogManagerTest.java",
      "line": "328",
      "body": "I added a name to this thread. The Scala version was not joining it and this was causing issues in Java. Having it named made me noticed the issue."
    },
    {
      "author": {
        "login": "mimaison"
      },
      "createdAt": "2026-04-24T13:12:13Z",
      "path": "storage/src/test/java/org/apache/kafka/storage/internals/log/LogManagerTest.java",
      "line": "1060",
      "body": "We have 2 log dirs and 2 recovery threads per log dirs, so 4 recovery threads are expected with their metrics. The original test only used 2 partitions to test all metrics but I think it's an issue with the Scala test.\n\nFirst by passing `mockMap` to `UnifiedLog.create()`, `numRemainingSegments` is not tracked properly, and all the metrics actually have `null` as their value. The Scala test pass because we retrieve the metrics as `Gauge[Int]` instances and Scala returns `0` when its auto unboxes an `Int` set to `null` to `0`.\n\nThis can be seen if we update  `verifyRemainingSegmentsToRecoverMetric()` with:\n```scala\n    // expected the end value is 0\n    logSegmentMetrics.foreach { gauge =>\n      println(s\"${gauge.value()}\")\n      assertEquals(0, gauge.value())\n    }\n```\nThis prints:\n```\nnull\nnull\nnull\nnull\n```\nBut the `assertEquals(0, gauge.value())` calls still pass!"
    },
    {
      "author": {
        "login": "chia7712"
      },
      "createdAt": "2026-04-28T17:11:58Z",
      "path": "storage/src/main/java/org/apache/kafka/storage/internals/log/LogManager.java",
      "line": "727",
      "body": "Do we really need to catch and unwrap this? It seems like we could just let it be thrown."
    },
    {
      "author": {
        "login": "chia7712"
      },
      "createdAt": "2026-04-28T17:21:34Z",
      "path": "storage/src/test/java/org/apache/kafka/storage/internals/log/LogManagerTest.java",
      "line": null,
      "body": "Would you mind providing a simple implementation to decouple the test scope dependencies between these two modules?\n```java\n    private static class SimpleConfigRepository implements ConfigRepository {\n        private final Map<ConfigResource, Properties> configs = new HashMap<>();\n\n        public static SimpleConfigRepository forTopic(String topic, String key, String value) {\n            Properties properties = new Properties();\n            properties.put(key, value);\n            return forTopic(topic, properties);\n        }\n\n        public static SimpleConfigRepository forTopic(String topic, Properties properties) {\n            SimpleConfigRepository repository = new SimpleConfigRepository();\n            repository.configs.put(new ConfigResource(ConfigResource.Type.TOPIC, topic), properties);\n            return repository;\n        }\n\n        @Override\n        public Properties config(ConfigResource configResource) {\n            return configs.getOrDefault(configResource, new Properties());\n        }\n    }\n```"
    },
    {
      "author": {
        "login": "mimaison"
      },
      "createdAt": "2026-04-29T10:42:17Z",
      "path": "storage/src/test/java/org/apache/kafka/storage/internals/log/LogManagerTest.java",
      "line": null,
      "body": "This is a good idea. The basic implementation is short and this decouples the modules. Updated"
    },
    {
      "author": {
        "login": "mimaison"
      },
      "createdAt": "2026-04-29T10:45:20Z",
      "path": "storage/src/main/java/org/apache/kafka/storage/internals/log/LogManager.java",
      "line": "727",
      "body": "This was there in the original Scala implementation. I'm afraid that if we just throw `ExecutionException` this may make some error messages less clear about the actual failure."
    }
  ]
}

